# Edited by Alexei Sheredega [sherry-DEH-gah]

# The editor thanks the following people for testing the questions: Yulia Archangelskaya [ar-KHANG-ell-sky-uh], Svetlana Alypova [ah-LIP-oh-vuh], Alexander Yermishkin [yerr-MISH-kin], Vitali Kunashko [coo-NAHSH-caw], Maxim Merzliakov [mers-lyah-KOFF], Evgenia Miller, Daniil Mordin, Polina Nasledskova [nass-LED-skaw-vuh], Artem Sapozhnikov [sah-PODGE-nick-off], Alexander Snovsky [SNOFF-ski], Elman Talybov [tah-LIB-off], Yana Khlustova [CLUE-stoh-vuh].

? Leonard Mlodinov [MLAW-dee-noff] writes about a man who grew rich after he took notice of a barely discernible imbalance. In which South European city did this happen?
! Monte Carlo [accepted answer: Monaco].
/ Joseph Jagger, a mechanical engineer, noticed that one roulette wheel favoured some numbers over the others and realized that these coincidences were not random and were due to a weight imbalance of the wheel. Jagger started betting on these exact numbers and won a substantial sum, until the wheel got changed. Monte Carlo is the biggest gambling centre of Europe.
^ Леонард Млодинов. Совершенная случайность, стр. 132 [Leonard Mlodinov, Completely Accidentally]
@ Alexei Sheredega

? The playwright George Ade wrote about the life of the downtrodden in Chicago. Before the London premiere of one play by Ade, the spectators were given a kind of THIS. Identify THIS, using a word with a hyphen in it.
! British-American dictionary [acceptable answers: American-British dictionary, or with «English» instead of «British»].
/ many words that had at the time become commonplace in America were unknown in Britain.
^ 
- en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Ade
- Rap Keyes. Hidden history of coined words
@ Alexei Sheredega

? In the middle of the last century, a group of officials was considering, among others, the designation «man-in-a-can», until someone thought of the story of Jason. Identify their final choice.
! astronaut[s].
/ In 1958, NASA created a special group that had to devise a name for the future space explorers. Among the tongue-in-cheek options there was man-in-a-can, which sounded like «mannequin». But eventually someone saw the similarities with the quest for the Golden Fleece, and suggested a word that echoed «argonaut».
^ 
- http://www.collectspace.com/review/kcstoever/gamble-essay6.pdf
- Loyd S. Swenson Jr., James M. Grimwood, Charles C. Alexander «This New Ocean: A History of Project Mercury»
@ Alexei Sheredega

? Let’s continue. The 1998 movie Meet Joe Black holds the unofficial record for the biggest number of viewers who bought the ticket but did not see the movie itself. The explanation is that the distributors didn’t see THIS as a potential problem. Identify THIS as precisely as possible.
! trailer for Star Wars Episode 1: The Phantom Menace [accept all answers that mention a trailer or a promo roll for Star Wars, Star Wars Ep 1, Phantom Menace]
/ The fans were so excited for the restart of the saga, that they were ready to visit theaters only for the trailer and then leave before the 3-hour feature started. The Phantom Menace became something of a menace for the distributors too. And this question continued the space.
^ 
- decider.com/2018/11/13/star-wars-the-phantom-menace-trailer-meet-joe-black/
- Chuck Klosterman. The Nineties: A Book
@ Alexei Sheredega

? In the first half of the 20th century Broadway musicals were a risky business. To save their productions, producers brought in patrons who would be nicknamed Charlies. Which word did we substitute in this question?
! angels.
/ The patrons became like guardian angels for the productions. Several decades later the term came over into business, where investors who fund start-ups at early stages also came to be called angels. The substitution hints at the film title Charlie’s Angels.
^ pureportal.strath.ac.uk/en/publications/business-angels
@ Alexei Sheredega

? This question used to contain 2 facts, but the author chose to keep only one. Maria Selvam, an activist, says that the Indian poor hope to visit the rich countries at least in a way. She means THIS. Identify THIS using exactly two words.
! kidney transplantation [accepted answers: kidney transplant, kidney trade; same with another body parts].
/ These individuals have almost no chance to visit a rich country on their own, but they have an opportunity to improve their living standard by becoming an organ donor for money. Just like real donors, this question donated one of its facts to another question.
^ Скотт Карни. Красный рынок. [Scott Carney. The Red Market.]
@ Alexei Sheredega

? The earliest newspapers focused on numerous ongoing wars, so they were often distributed together with THIS. One of the first examples of THIS has an image of a figure with its back bent beneath its burden. Identify THIS using exactly one word.
! Atlas.
/ Readers had trouble finding their way through the many geographical names in the campaign descriptions, so a map collection came in handy. The word «atlas» for a map book was chosen after Atlas, the figure in Greek myth who held the skies on his shoulders.
^ 
- Эндрю Петтигри. Изобретение новостей. Как мир узнал о самом себе. [Andrew Pettegree. The Invention of News: How the World Came to Know About Itself]
- https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mercator_Hondius_Atlas_Minor_of_1607_frontispiece.png
@ Alexei Sheredega

? [Having read the answers out loud, read the acceptance criteria, given in italics.]
Attention! This is a double question. There will be 2 questions with 30 seconds’ thinking time for each. You must choose which of the two questions to answer. Answering both questions is not allowed.
1. In the times of racial segregation, the managers of one city utility enterprise in the state of Mississippi received a suggestion to prohibit sharing of THIS between white and black families. Identify THIS using exactly two words.
2. In the movie Pillow Talk, complaints about THIS draw characters closer to each other. Identify THIS using exactly two words.
! 
- Shared line
- Shared line
The answer card must have exactly one answer «shared line» (or equivalent, see below) to question 1 or question 2 of the double. If the phrase «shared line» (or equivalent) is written twice, the answer is not accepted.
[accepted answers: party line, shared telephone, party wire, multiparty line]
/ Shared lines, or party lines, were built in such a way that only one subscriber in the group could use the phone at any moment. The other subscriber(s) could eavesdrop on the call though, which was a frequent cause of complaints and arguments. And just like a shared line does not allow answering two calls simultaneously, in this double question you were only allowed to answer one of the two questions.
^ 
- https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=h_UlAAAAIBAJ&pg=820,5514413
- en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pillow_Talk_(film)
@ Alexei Sheredega

? The X in this question substitutes one word.
The comedian Milton Berle was sarcastic about the show business practices in the early years of the last century. In his routine he said that he laughed so hard at another comedian’s performance, that he almost dropped his X. In which country did an X falling down count as a positive result?
! South Africa.
/ The X stands for «pencil». Milton Berle’s sarcasm was aimed at comedians stealing jokes. In South Africa, in the years of apartheid, a so-called «pencil test» was used to determine «racial purity». If a pencil didn’t get stuck in a person’s hair and fell down, the individual thus «tested» was counted as a white and their rights weren’t infringed.
^ 
- Lawrence J. Epstein. The Haunted Smile: The Story Of Jewish Comedians In America (https://clck.ru/32TUk5)
- ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Карандашный_тест
@ Alexei Sheredega

? Maxim Gorky wrote that on weekends Americans go to amusement parks that poison their souls. Developing Gorky’s thought, John Kasson writes that for Americans THIS was actually amusement parks. Identify THIS using four words exactly.
! opiate for the masses [accepted answers: opium/opiate of/for the people/masses, but only answers exactly matching this template].
/ According to Gorky, amusement parks were America’s substitute for religion, but like religion, they took workers’ attention away from class struggle. On his trip to the USA, Gorky spent a whole day on Coney Island rides and then wrote his scathing article.
^ 
- John F. Kasson. Amusing the million: Coney Island at the turn of the century
- http://gorkiy-lit.ru/gorkiy/articles/article-367-2.htm
@ Alexei Sheredega

? Handout [
(img 2022-2023_III_en_001.jpeg)
]
In the USSR, extrasensory abilities were not believed to exist, until the emergence of news stories about Djuna the clairvoyant. As early as in the 80s, the poet Robert Rozhdestvensky [rozh-DEST-venn-ski] compared Djuna to THIS. Identify THIS using exactly two words.
! black swan
/ The term «black swan» denotes an improbable development that suddenly becomes real. The term became popular much later than this, thanks to Nassim Taleb’s 2007 book, so this is most likely coincidental. Djuna’s dress on the picture might have reminded you of the black tutu in the movie Black Swan.
^ 
- https://djvu.online/file/CeDJ6aVULBbhN
- ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Чёрный_лебедь_(теория)
@ Alexei Sheredega

? There are substituted words in this question. The scientist Paul Kammerer used to sit outside for days and write down how many people passed him by, how they were dressed, whether they met someone they knew. Kammerer wanted to prove that events are not fictitious. Which two words did we substitute in this question?
! similarities, coincidental [accepted answers: for the first word, resemblances]
/ Kammerer was certain that all coincidences can be boiled down to specific laws and tried to formulate them empirically. The substitution is based on the usual disclaimer that all events depicted are fictitious and similarities are coincidental. We started the block with a question about coincidental roulette wins and finished with a question about coincidence.
^ Джозеф Мазур. Игра случая. Математика и мифология совпадения [Joseph Mazur. Fluke: The Math and Myth of Coincidence]
@ Alexei Sheredega

